*.log
npm-debug.log*

# Build artifacts
dist/
build/
.build/
*.bun-build
.*.bun-build

# Claude Code session state — with ONE exception.
#
# `.claude/` holds machine-local session and settings files that must never be
# committed, but this repository DOES ship its slash-command wrappers: they are
# the discoverable entry point to every mise task, and a wrapper that only
# exists on one machine is not an entry point at all.
#
# The two lines below are the only form of this that works. A bare `.claude/`
# followed by `!.claude/commands/` does NOT re-include anything: git never
# descends into an excluded directory, so a negation under it is unreachable.
# Excluding the CONTENTS with `.claude/*` leaves the directory itself
# traversable, and the negation then applies.
.claude/*
!.claude/commands/

# Local mise configuration. Holds machine-specific paths and API tokens; a
# committed copy is a published copy.
.mise.toml

# Lint / link-check result caches
.lint-relative-paths-results.txt
.lychee-results.json
.lychee-results.txt
.lycheecache
.link-check-results.md

# Python / Rust caches
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.venv/

# Temporary workspace
tmp/

# Exactly one lockfile ships, and it is bun.lock.
#
# Two lockfiles in one repository is not redundancy, it is ambiguity: they
# resolve independently, drift apart without anything noticing, and the next
# person to debug an install has to work out which one their tooling actually
# read. The reference marketplace this repo was modelled on shipped both and
# had no answer to that question.
#
# If you prefer npm, delete bun.lock, remove this block, and commit
# package-lock.json instead — but ship one, not both.
package-lock.json
pnpm-lock.yaml
yarn.lock
